    "# Sprint 1: Supervised Prediction - Group Report"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9ac6ff4f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Project Overview & Research Objectives\n",
    "The aim of this project is to investigate how effectively different machine learning approaches can estimate the probability of a football shot resulting in a goal. We compare four modelling techniques using probabilistic performance measures, evaluating their predictive accuracy and calibration on an unseen season. We then use the best-performing model to examine spatial patterns in shot quality and investigate how expected goals relate to actual scoring outcomes.\n",
    "\n",
    "This establishes three clear objectives for our analysis:\n",
    "1. **Develop** different approaches to estimating expected goals ($xG$).\n",
    "2. **Compare** their predictive performance using appropriate statistical measures (strictly proper scoring rules).\n",
    "3. **Apply** the resulting predictions to investigate spatial patterns in football shooting and team performance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "math-definition-xg",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Mathematical Definition of Expected Goals ($xG$)\n",
    "To formalise the prediction problem, let the binary outcome variable for individual shot $i$ be defined as:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Y_i =\n",
    "\\begin{cases}\n",
    "1, & \\text{if shot }i\\text{ results in a goal},\\\\\n",
    "0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n",
    "\\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We define expected goals ($xG$) as the conditional probability of a goal given the available feature set:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "xG_i = P(Y_i=1\\mid X_i),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "where $X_i$ represents the vector of available information regarding the shot, such as spatial coordinates (location, distance, angle) and situational context (e.g., shot type, game state).\n",
    "\n",
    "This formulation establishes that $xG$ is not a subjective metric of shot quality, but rather an objective probability estimated from historical observations. It is vital to distinguish between individual and aggregate interpretations:\n",
    "* A single shot with an $xG$ of $0.3$ possesses a $30\\%$ estimated probability of resulting in a goal.\n",
    "* A collection of $100$ independent shots, each carrying an $xG$ of $0.3$, would be expected to yield approximately $30$ goals in total."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "data-framework",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data and Prediction Framework\n",
    "The dataset was obtained via **Understat**, representing individual football shots across multiple European league seasons. The response variable is binary ($Y_i$), indicating whether a shot resulted in a goal, and the explanatory variables ($X_i$) capture comprehensive spatial coordinates and match contexts.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rather than allocating observations randomly, we implemented a rigorous **chronological split** across seasons:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Dataset Split | Seasons Covered | Purpose ||\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Training** | 2021/22 -- 2023/24 | Fit model parameters ||\n| **Validation** | 2024/25 | Model selection and tuning ||\n| **Testing** | 2025/26 | Final out-of-sample evaluation |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Methodological Justification\n",
    "We used a chronological split rather than random cross-validation to better reflect the real-world application of an $xG$ model: predictions must be made using historical information to estimate future outcomes. Reserving the entire 2025/26 season as an unseen test period ensures an honest, out-of-sample evaluation free from data leakage."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "evaluation-metrics",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Evaluation Metrics and Proper Scoring Rules\n",
    "To evaluate our probabilistic predictions, we utilise three complementary metrics:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Brier Score\n",
    "The Brier score measures the mean squared difference between predicted probabilities ($f_i$) and observed outcomes ($y_i$):\n",
    "$$\n",
    "BS = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(f_i-y_i)^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "It is bounded between $0$ and $1$ for binary outcomes (lower values indicate better calibration). It is a strictly proper scoring rule, meaning a model minimises its expected score by reporting its true estimated probabilities.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Log Loss\n",
    "Log loss evaluates the information-theoretic penalty across predictions:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "LL = -\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left[y_i\\log(f_i)+(1-y_i)\\log(1-f_i)\\right].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Log loss heavily penalises *confident incorrect predictions*. Taking the derivative with respect to a predicted probability $f_i$ when $y_i=1$,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial LL}{\\partial f_i} = -\\frac{1}{f_i},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "which approaches negative infinity (its magnitude approaches infinity) as $f_i \\rightarrow 0$. Like the Brier score, log loss is a strictly proper scoring rule.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. ROC-AUC\n",
    "ROC-AUC measures a model's capacity to discriminate between goals and non-goals across all classification thresholds ($0.5$ for random ranking up to $1.0$ for perfect discrimination). While Brier score and log loss assess probability calibration, ROC-AUC evaluates ranking performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why Use All Three?\n",
    "Using these complementary metrics provides a holistic evaluation: Brier score measures overall squared error, log loss penalises severe overconfidence, and ROC-AUC evaluates discrimination independent of probability scaling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "class-imbalance",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Class Imbalance and Null Model Baseline\n",
    "Standard classification accuracy is deeply misleading for probabilistic predictions due to severe class imbalance. Across our training dataset, historical goal frequency is approximately $10\\%$.\n",
    "\n",
    "If a naive baseline model predicted zero ($f_i = 0$) for every single shot, it would achieve a superficial classification accuracy of:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{0 + 0.9N}{N} = 90\\%.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Despite a $90\\%$ accuracy rate, this trivial baseline possesses **zero predictive utility**. Furthermore, it would yield a poor Brier score (matching the base variance $\\approx 0.10$) and an **infinite log loss** because it assigns zero probability to events that actually occur ($y_i=1$). \n",
    "\n",
    "To benchmark our four advanced machine learning models (Random Forests, Neural Networks, Linear Regression, and Gradient Boosting), we compare them against both this constant-probability null model and the established external **Understat benchmark**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "understat-benchmark",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The Understat Benchmark\n",
    "We evaluate our models against the existing $xG$ estimates provided by Understat. Rather than treating Understat as an infallible, objectively optimal gold standard, we use it as an established external benchmark to test whether our independently trained models can achieve comparable or superior predictive performance on unseen data."
   ]
